@* The phone's dialog: a sheet that rises from the bottom edge over a dimmed page. Modal —
   the scrim covers the page and the tab bar, and two focus guards wrap Tab inside the sheet —
   and it always closes four ways: Done, Escape, a tap on the scrim, or a drag down. The drag is
   pointer-only, so the handle stays aria-hidden; keyboard and screen readers use the rest. The owner returns
   focus to whatever opened it; the sheet cannot know what that was. *@

<div class="sheet-layer">
    @* A real button so a tap anywhere outside the sheet closes it. Out of the tab order: the
       keyboard closes with Escape or Done, and a focus stop outside a modal dialog would
       break the trap the guards make. *@
    <button type="button" class="sheet-scrim" @ref="_scrimElement" aria-label="Close" tabindex="-1" @onclick="CloseAsync"></button>

    <section class="sheet"
             role="dialog"
             aria-modal="true"
             aria-label="@AriaLabel"
             @ref="_sheetElement"
             @onkeydown="OnKeyDownAsync">

        <span class="sheet__guard" tabindex="0" @onfocus="FocusLastAsync"></span>

        <span class="sheet__handle" aria-hidden="true"></span>

        @ChildContent

        <button type="button" class="secondary button--thumb sheet__done" @onclick="CloseAsync">Done</button>

        <span class="sheet__guard" tabindex="0" @onfocus="FocusFirstAsync"></span>
    </section>
</div>
